WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.339
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online IELTS preparation.

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:16.192
Unit eight: Reading: Matching Headings. Today you will match each paragraph to the heading that gives its main idea.

4
00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.272
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.547
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.384
Paragraph.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:29.497
Paragraph means a section of text about one idea.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:31.769
Read the first paragraph.

9
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:35.187
Your turn. Say: Paragraph.

10
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.058
Paragraph.

11
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:40.551
Heading.

12
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.014
Heading means a short title for a paragraph.

13
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.071
Choose the best heading.

14
00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:49.080
Your turn. Say: Heading.

15
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:52.795
Heading.

16
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.644
Main point.

17
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.126
Main point means the most important idea.

18
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:00.797
The main point is in the first line.

19
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:04.140
Your turn. Say: Main point.

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:07.989
Main point.

21
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.844
Extra heading.

22
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.744
Extra heading means a heading that matches no paragraph.

23
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.384
There are more headings than paragraphs.

24
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:19.659
Your turn. Say: Extra heading.

25
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.698
Extra heading.

26
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.184
Theme.

27
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:27.986
Theme means the general subject.

28
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:30.142
The theme is education.

29
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.938
Your turn. Say: Theme.

30
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.372
Theme.

31
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:38.060
Overview.

32
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.084
Overview means a general summary.

33
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.497
Give an overview of the topic.

34
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:46.492
Your turn. Say: Overview.

35
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:50.358
Overview.

36
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:52.083
Contrast.

37
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:55.896
Contrast means a difference between two things.

38
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:58.309
The writer shows a contrast.

39
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.511
Your turn. Say: Contrast.

40
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:05.426
Contrast.

41
00:02:06.183 --> 00:02:07.150
Eliminate.

42
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.614
Eliminate means to remove a wrong option.

43
00:02:11.217 --> 00:02:13.205
Eliminate the headings you have used.

44
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.469
Your turn. Say: Eliminate.

45
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:20.346
Eliminate.

46
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:24.854
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:28.285
Visal, how do I match headings quickly?

48
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:38.839
Read the headings first, then read the topic sentence of each paragraph. Choose the heading that matches the main point, not just one detail.

49
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.343
Why are some headings wrong even though they share words with the text?

50
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:54.498
Headings are often written to trap you. A paragraph might mention money, but its main point is about learning, so a heading about money would be wrong.

51
00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:56.921
What if I am stuck?

52
00:02:58.017 --> 00:03:07.078
Skip it and return later. Eliminate headings you have already used, and remember there are always more headings than paragraphs.

53
00:03:08.233 --> 00:03:10.210
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.731
Grammar focus: Strategy: choose the main point, not a detail.

55
00:03:16.031 --> 00:03:17.218
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:21.634
The main point is usually in the first or last sentence of the paragraph.

57
00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.639
A heading with a shared word can be a trap if it matches only a detail.

58
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:36.266
Read the headings, then match each paragraph by its main point. Be careful of words repeated from the text, because they may point to a detail rather than the whole idea.

59
00:03:37.017 --> 00:03:41.190
Be careful! Don't say: I match the heading to one detail.

60
00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:46.108
Say: I match the heading to the main point.

61
00:03:46.408 --> 00:03:47.932
Choose the main point.

62
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.374
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.644
A Matching Task.

64
00:03:53.944 --> 00:03:57.847
A passage about city life has five paragraphs and eight headings.

65
00:03:58.833 --> 00:04:02.447
The first paragraph mentions money, but its main point is about housing.

66
00:04:03.433 --> 00:04:06.536
The teacher reminds students to eliminate used headings.

67
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:10.264
Three headings are extra and match no paragraph.

68
00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.823
The class discusses why one heading is a trap.

69
00:04:14.933 --> 00:04:19.051
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:22.183
The main point is usually found blank.

71
00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:27.263
A, in a number. B, in the title page. C, in a topic sentence.

72
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.659
The answer is C: in a topic sentence.

73
00:04:34.817 --> 00:04:37.199
There are blank headings than paragraphs.

74
00:04:37.499 --> 00:04:41.211
A, more. B, fewer. C, the same number of.

75
00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:46.302
The answer is A: more.

76
00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.088
A heading matching only a detail is blank.

77
00:04:50.388 --> 00:04:54.645
A, a title. B, often wrong. C, always right.

78
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.501
The answer is B: often wrong.

79
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.197
What should you do when stuck?

80
00:05:03.497 --> 00:05:08.056
A, stop the test. B, guess the same letter. C, skip and return.

81
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.018
The answer is C: skip and return.

82
00:05:15.183 --> 00:05:17.624
What is the first paragraph mainly about?

83
00:05:17.924 --> 00:05:21.136
A, housing. B, food. C, sport.

84
00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:26.955
The answer is A: housing.

85
00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:31.260
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:32.117 --> 00:05:34.128
How do you choose the correct heading?

87
00:05:42.917 --> 00:05:44.324
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:44.624 --> 00:05:59.528
I read all the headings first and then read the topic sentence of each paragraph. I choose the heading that gives the main point of the whole paragraph, not only one detail. I eliminate headings I have already used.

89
00:05:59.828 --> 00:06:01.525
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:08.285
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: choose the main point, not a detail.

91
00:06:08.585 --> 00:06:13.002
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

92
00:06:13.302 --> 00:06:15.641
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

93
00:06:15.941 --> 00:06:18.616
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

